  "clinical_tradeoff_analysis": "The frozen NIH thresholds were calibrated to enforce high specificity (84.2% - 97.0%) to minimize false positives in low-prevalence screening populations. However, this high specificity threshold policy inevitably forces low sensitivity (13.7% - 50.1%), resulting in false negative rates of 49.9% to 86.3% on the NIH benchmark itself. When applied to external test images with subtle or non-massive disease (such as Atelectasis.jpg, Pneumothorax.jpeg, and Cardiomegaly.jpg), the model outputs moderate-to-high activation probabilities that fall just below these conservative thresholds, yielding the observed negative flags."
